 t\n報名/票券: https://appointments.library.stanford.edu/event/17680622
 \n\nJoin us for "Two Views of the Chinese Coast\," a talk by Kären Wigen\
 , the Frances & Charles Field Professor in Stanford's Department of Histor
 y\, at the David Rumsey Map Center. When cartographic collections began to
  appear online in the 1990s\, historians of East Asia were quick to seize 
 on maps as subjects for research. Over the next thirty years\, a sophistic
 ated field of critical map studies developed\, illuminating the lively lan
 dscape of cartographic production across the early modern Sinosphere. Whil
 e map-focused research made rapid strides\, a gap gradually opened between
  cartographic research (the history of maps) and what we might call map-in
 formed history (history with maps). \n\nThis talk attempts to address that
  gap by investigating two works from Stanford University Libraries’ coll
 ections: a Qing-era scroll painting of the Pacific coast\, housed in the D
 avid Rumsey Map Collection\, and a British map of the same shoreline from 
 1859\, owned by a private collector and held digitally within the Barry La
 wrence Ruderman Map Collection. During the 19th century\, both maps served
  as tools of military planning\; where the Qing scroll was likely consulte
 d for coastal defense\, the British chart was designed for a naval attack.
  By putting them in dialogue with each other\, the talk suggests how cross
 -cultural map comparison can help us tell more complex\, entangled stories
  about Euro-Asian interaction.\n\nSpeaker Bio\n\nKären Wigen teaches Japa
 nese history and the history of cartography. A ge
